# slatwall-ts — ignore rules for the TypeScript subtree
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL
#
# The repository root already carries a `.gitignore`, but it predates this
# subtree and is one of the untouched CFML-era files this port must not modify
# (AAP 0.9.5: "git status shows only additions under slatwall-ts/"; AAP 0.4.1:
# "Zero existing repository files are UPDATEd"). It ignores none of the four
# artefact classes a Node/TypeScript build produces — not `node_modules/`, not
# `dist/`, not coverage output, and not `.env`. (Read it: the entries are
# `*.project`, `*.settings`, `*.zip`, `WEB-INF`, `logs/` and the like — a
# ColdFusion-era list.)
#
# Two mandated gates depend on the rules below:
#
#   * AAP 0.9.5 — "Zero existing repository files are modified. `git status`
#     shows only additions under `slatwall-ts/`." Without these rules the
#     installed dependency tree (tens of thousands of files), the emitted Lambda
#     bundle and the coverage report all appear as untracked additions, and the
#     gate cannot be evaluated, let alone pass.
#
#   * AAP 0.8.3 / 0.9.5 — "Environment-driven configuration with no hardcoded
#     credentials, accompanied by a committed `.env.example` documenting the
#     contract" and "No credential is hardcoded." `slatwall-ts/.env.example` is
#     the committed contract and carries no secret; a developer's real `.env`
#     carries `DB_PASSWORD` and must never become committable. The `.env`
#     /`.env.*` pair with the `!.env.example` re-inclusion is what keeps the
#     contract tracked and the secret untracked.
#
# ★★★ THIS FILE WAS REMOVED AND HAS NOW BEEN RESTORED, AND BOTH TURNS ARE ON THE
# RECORD RATHER THAN LEFT TO A COMMIT MESSAGE.
#
#   REMOVED: a code review deleted it on the ground that AAP 0.3.1 enumerates
#   the subtree root as exactly TWELVE artifacts and "a thirteenth cannot be
#   admitted by being useful". The rules did not vanish with it — they were
#   re-declared as `UNTRACKED_ROOT_NAMES` in
#   `tests/traceability/legacyTestMap.ts` (so the census still knows which root
#   names are generated) and re-applied through this checkout's
#   `.git/info/exclude`.
#
#   RESTORED: the project-wide final security assessment raised that state as
#   SEC-G (LOW, CWE-200/CWE-540) and required a COMMITTED ignore mechanism,
#   because `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone and never travels: a FRESH CLONE
#   has no rules at all, so a developer following this README can create `.env`
#   with `DB_PASSWORD` in it and stage it, or stage `dist/`'s bundles and source
#   maps.
#
#   WHAT AUTHORIZES IT, STATED PRECISELY, BECAUSE AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS
#   NOTE OVERSTATED IT. It read: "AAP 0.4.4 and 0.2.1 also state the target as
#   trailing PATTERNS, and `legacyTestMap.ts` publishes the mechanism for exactly
#   this case: `recordedScopeAdditions` itemises a sanctioned addition." A later
#   review checked that claim against the plan and rejected it: every pattern
#   AAP 0.4.4 lists is a trailing wildcard over a DIRECTORY, and the subtree root
#   is given as an INSTANCE list of twelve artifacts with no root pattern at all.
#   So no plan pattern sanctions this file, and the register row that claimed one
#   named a pseudo-pattern — `slatwall-ts/<root artifact>` — that appears nowhere
#   in the plan.
#
#   THE HONEST AUTHORITY IS THE SECURITY DECISION ITSELF. This file is admitted
#   as ONE EXACT, CLOSED EXCEPTION to the frozen root enumeration, recorded in
#   `legacyTestMap.ts` as the single entry of `ROOT_SCOPE_EXCEPTIONS`, keyed by
#   this filename and naming SEC-G as its ground. `recordedScopeAdditions` still
#   itemises the path, so the tree is reconciled LOUDLY against the frozen
#   165-file census rather than silently drifting past it, and the `A18` block
#   fails if this row is ever removed while the file stays, or the file removed
#   while the row stays. It also fails if a SECOND root filename is added, because
#   the exception table's key set is asserted to be exactly this one name.
#
#   IT REMAINS AN OPEN ITEM FOR THE PLAN OWNER. Ratifying a thirteenth root
#   artifact into AAP 0.3.1 — or reconciling the root back to twelve and closing
#   SEC-G another way — is a scope decision this port may not take for itself.
#   Until it is taken, the exception is what a reviewer sees, by name.
#
# The rules are deliberately scoped to this subtree. Nothing here can affect the
# CFML tree, because a `.gitignore` applies to its own directory and below.
#
# Eleven rules, and `UNTRACKED_ROOT_NAMES` in `tests/traceability/legacyTestMap.ts`
# declares nine of them. The two it does not are `.vitest/`, a directory rule the
# root census never reaches because it censuses files only, and `!.env.example`,
# the single negation - a name the census must keep VISIBLE because it is a frozen
# root artifact. The `A18` block checks the agreement in BOTH directions: every
# name the census calls generated is a rule here, and every rule here that can
# match a root FILE is a name the census calls generated. Without the second
# direction a `.env.local` would be gitignored and simultaneously reported as
# scope drift.

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output — `npm run bundle` / `npm run package` write here (esbuild.config.mjs)
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test / coverage output
coverage/

# Tooling caches and local logs. Written as globs, not exact names, and each glob
# is one of the names `UNTRACKED_ROOT_NAMES` declares in
# `tests/traceability/legacyTestMap.ts` - a traceability case asserts that
# agreement, because a census that thinks a file is generated while this file does
# not would report a build product as scope drift.
*.eslintcache
*.log
.vitest/

# Local environment (the committed contract is .env.example)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
